/*Q6. Write a Java program to demonstrate classes and objects by implementing a simple Employee Management System for a medium-sized IT
company. The system stores employee records and calculates gross salary by adding allowances to the basic salary.
Class: Employee
Create a class named Employee with the following instance variables:
• name (String): Stores the employee's name.
• id (int): Stores the employee's ID.
• department (String): Stores the employee's department.
• basicSalary (double): Stores the employee's basic salary.
Implement the following:
1. A parameterized constructor Employee(String name, int id, String department, double basicSalary) to initialize all four instance variables
using the this keyword.
2. Amethod calculateGrossSalary() that calculates and returns the gross salary using the following formula:
Gross Salary = Basic Salary + HRA + DA
• HRA= 20% of Basic Salary
- DA= 10% of Basic Salary
3. A method display() that prints the employee's details in the following order, with each field on a separate line:
Employee ID: <id>
Name: <name>
Department: <department>
Basic Salary: <basic_salary>
Gross Salary: <gross_salary>
Both salary values must be formatted to one decimal place.
Class: EmployeeManagementSystem
The EmployeeManagementSystem class should:
1. Read the number of employees.
2. Create an array of Employee objects of the specified size.
3. Read each employee's name, ID, department, and basic salary, in that order.
4. Create an Employee object for each record and store it in the array.
5. Call display() for every employee to display their details.
Input Format:
1. The first line contains an integer n, representing the number of employees.
2. The next 7 lines each contain:
• A string representing Employee Name
• An integer representing Employee ID
• A string representing Department
• A double representing Basic Salary
These values are entered on separate lines for each employee in the given order.
Output Format:
• For each employee, display:
Employee ID: <id>
Name: <name>
Department: <department>
Basic Salary: <basic_salary>
Gross Salary: <gross_salary>
Note:
• The Basic Salary should be formated to one decimal places.
• The Gross Salary should be formated to one decimal places.
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class Employee {
    String name;
    int id;
    String department;
    double basicSalary;
 
    Employee(String name, int id, String department, double basicSalary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.id = id;
        this.department = department;
        this.basicSalary = basicSalary;
    }
 
    double calculateGrossSalary() {
        double hra = 0.20 * basicSalary;
        double da = 0.10 * basicSalary;
        return basicSalary + hra + da;
    }
 
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Employee ID: " + id);
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Department: " + department);
        System.out.printf("Basic Salary: %.1f%n", basicSalary);
        System.out.printf("Gross Salary: %.1f%n", calculateGrossSalary());
    }
}
 
public class EmployeeManagementSystem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
        Employee[] emps = new Employee[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            String name = sc.nextLine().trim();
            int id = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
            String dept = sc.nextLine().trim();
            double basic = Double.parseDouble(sc.nextLine().trim());
            emps[i] = new Employee(name, id, dept, basic);
        }
        for (Employee e : emps) {
            e.display();
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}
